course: dictionary
lesson: 10931
title: Vow: a solemn promise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vow is a serious promise that you make firmly and mean to keep. «A vow is a solemn promise, often made before God and other people.» Solemn means very serious and important, so a vow is more than a casual promise like «I'll try to be on time».
explain: Vow can be a noun or a verb. As a noun it is the promise itself: «She made a vow.» As a verb, to vow is to promise firmly that you will do something. Look at the words around it. «Make», «take» and «break» often go with vow, as in «break a vow», which means you failed to keep it.
explain: Vows are part of weddings and church life. «At a church wedding the couple make vows before God and witnesses.» Witnesses are people who watch and remember what happens. Wedding vows are the promises a couple make when they marry, and monks and nuns take vows too.
fact: A vow is a serious, firm promise.
fact: To vow is a verb: to promise firmly that you will do something.
fact: Useful phrases: «take a vow», «make a vow», «break a vow», «wedding vows».
fact: An oath is a close synonym: a promise to tell the truth or keep your word, sometimes «sworn» on a holy book.
fact: A good check is to swap in promise. If the sentence still makes sense and sounds more serious, vow fits well.
exercise: «The hero vowed to rescue his friend.» What does vowed mean here? | Forgot to do something | Ate a big meal | *Firmly promised to do something | Shouted loudly at someone
exercise: What are wedding vows? | The music played at the start | Gifts for the guests | *Serious promises a couple make when they marry | Flowers thrown at the church door
exercise: «She will vow never to lie again.» What part of speech is vow here? | Adjective | *Verb | Noun | Adverb
test: Which phrase means you failed to keep a promise? | *Break a vow | Wedding vows | Take a vow | Make a vow
test: What does solemn mean? | Very silly and loud | *Very serious and important | Very cold | Very light and quick
exam: Ella says vow is just a fancy word for wish. What is the best reply? | *No, a vow is a serious promise you mean to keep | No, a vow is a type of cake | Yes, they mean exactly the same | Yes, a wish is always a promise
